# WHICH FILES THE PIPELINE REWRITES, AND WHY THAT CHANGES WHAT A CONFLICT MEANS
#
# 2026-09-07, 23:40. The registries run read twelve state rolls, surveyed
# sixty-seven candidate pages, geocoded 894 addresses and built a directory of
# 4,696 elevators — thirty-six minutes — committed 2,111 businesses, and then:
#
#     ! [rejected]        main -> main (fetch first)
#     somebody pushed first (attempt 1 of 4); rebasing
#     Auto-merging data/directory.json
#     CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in data/directory.json
#     ##[error]the work is committed locally and NOT on the remote
#
# The barchart job had pushed during those thirty-six minutes. Both jobs run
# scripts/build_directory.mjs, both rewrite data/directory.json in full, and a
# three-way merge of two independently regenerated JSON files conflicts every
# time they overlap. Everything the run learned died on the runner.
#
# scripts/commit-and-push.sh already carries two paragraphs about this exact
# shape of loss — a rejected push in August, an untracked file in August. Both
# were fixed. Neither covered a CONTENT conflict, because until two workflows
# regenerated the same derived file there had never been one.
#
# THE POINT. For a hand-written file a conflict is information: two people
# changed the same line and somebody has to decide. For a file the pipeline
# regenerates wholesale from its inputs, a conflict is NOISE — neither side is
# "right", both are snapshots of a build, and the next run of either job
# overwrites whatever is there. Merging them line by line produces something
# neither build would ever have written.
#
# So these paths take the version being replayed, and the loss is stated rather
# than pretended away: the other job's contribution to that derived file is
# dropped until the next rebuild picks it up from its own committed inputs,
# which are NOT in this list and are never resolved this way. A transient gap in
# a derived file against thirty-six minutes of harvest is not a close call.
#
# sources/ IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. A source manifest is edited, not
# regenerated — geocode-fill fills a coordinate, stamp-country writes a country,
# the sweeps add new files. Two jobs disagreeing about one manifest is a real
# disagreement and it must still stop everything and ask.

data/*.json            merge=generated
data/gaps/*            merge=generated
geocodes/*.json        merge=generated
probe-lists/*.txt      merge=generated

